# The glossary: what the words the manual uses mean. Link to a term from any text with
# [[its name]].

Term: ACRO
  The rate mode of the sim. The sticks set the rate of roll and pitch. When you centre the
  sticks, the plane holds its attitude. Use it for aerobatics.

Term: ANGLE
  The self-levelling mode of the sim. The sticks set the angle of bank and pitch. When you
  release the sticks, the plane becomes level. Use it when you learn to fly.

Term: ArduPilot
  The open-source autopilot. ArduPlane is its version for planes. Here, it runs as
  [[SITL]] and flies the plane of the sim. The [[Ground control station]] talks to it.

Term: Annunciator
  A lamp on the CAUTION / WARNING screen of the ground station. ARMED lights when the plane
  is armed. Other lamps show the link, GPS, the battery, a stall and the fence.

Term: AUTO
  The mode of ArduPilot that flies the [[mission]] on the vehicle, one item after the other.

Term: Chase
  The view from just behind the plane, following it.

Term: CRUISE
  The mode of ArduPilot for forward flight that holds the height and the heading. The sticks steer
  the plane, and the autopilot does the rest. Use it to travel.

Term: Current item
  The [[mission]] item that the plane flies to in AUTO. The map shows it in magenta. WP1
  sends the plane back to the first item.

Term: Differential thrust
  When you use the rudder, one motor turns faster and the other turns more slowly. Thus the
  plane turns more quickly in yaw than with the rudder only. X turns it on and off.

Term: FBWA
  Fly By Wire A, a mode of ArduPilot. The sticks set the angle of bank and pitch, and the
  autopilot holds them. It is like ANGLE in the sim.

Term: FPV
  First-person view: the picture from a camera in the nose of the plane. The camera points
  20° up.

Term: GUIDED
  The mode of ArduPilot that flies the plane to one point. GOTO on the map puts the plane in
  GUIDED.

Term: Hand launch
  A launch where you throw the plane into the air. The plane does not use a runway. You
  launch the T1-S Ranger in this way, and also the plane in the sim.

Term: HUD
  The text on the view of the sim. The top line shows the flight mode, the rates and
  differential thrust. The
  lines below it show the controller, the view, the throttle, the speeds, the height, the
  attitude, the battery and the wind. Messages such as CRASHED and PAUSED (P) appear in the
  middle.

Term: Home
  The point where you armed the plane. RTL brings the plane back to it. The map shows it.

Term: Line of sight
  The view from the [[pilot box]]. You look at the plane as you do at the field. The HUD
  calls it LINE OF SIGHT, and the help screen calls it LOS.

Term: LOITER
  A mode of ArduPilot, a mission item and a [[primitive]] of an [[agent plan]], number 6. Each
  one circles a point. The loiter primitive circles the place where the plane got the plan.

Term: MANUAL
  In the sim, the mode where the sticks move the control surfaces directly. Nothing helps
  you. In ArduPilot, the mode where the autopilot sends the sticks directly to the plane.

Term: MAVLink
  The protocol that ArduPilot and ground stations use to talk to each other.

Term: Remote ID
  A broadcast that an unmanned aircraft sends on Bluetooth or Wi-Fi. It says who the aircraft
  is and where it is, about once a second. Any receiver near the aircraft can read it. The
  standard is ASTM F3411. Many countries ask for it.

Term: Telemetry link
  The radio link that brings the data of the plane to the station. The station gets it from
  the TX15. The link is narrow, so the plane sends its own data first.

Term: Marker
  The symbol of another plane on the [[tactical map]]. A live marker is bright. A stale marker
  is dim and stays where the plane was last heard.

Term: Mission
  The list of items that the autopilot flies in AUTO: waypoints, loiters, a takeoff, a
  landing or a return home. The map shows the mission on the vehicle as a solid line.

Term: Pilot box
  The place where you stand at the field to fly. The sim starts there, with the plane in
  your hand.

Term: Pitch stick
  The stick that moves the nose up and down. On the keyboard, W pushes it forward (nose down)
  and S pulls it back (nose up).

Term: Plan
  The mission that you edit in the ground station. The map shows it as a dashed line. When
  you upload it, it becomes the mission on the vehicle. In [[Flying by plan]], a plan is an
  [[agent plan]].

Term: Plan altitude
  The height above home for new waypoints. It shows between ALT- and ALT+.

Term: Roll stick
  The stick that banks the plane. On the keyboard, A rolls the plane to the left and D rolls
  it to the right.

Term: QHOVER
  A hover mode of ArduPilot for a [[VTOL]]. The throttle stick sets the climb or the descent. The
  plane does not hold its place, so the wind moves it.

Term: QLOITER
  A hover mode of ArduPilot for a [[VTOL]] that also holds the plane at one place. Use it to take
  off and land.

Term: QRTL
  The mode of ArduPilot for a [[VTOL]] that flies the plane to [[home]] and lands it straight down.

Term: Rates
  How far the control surfaces move for a movement of the stick. High rates give tight
  turns. Low rates give gentle turns. L changes between them.

Term: RTL
  Return to launch: ArduPilot flies the plane back to [[home]]. RTL is also a mission item
  that does the same.

Term: Sensor feed
  The window that shows the picture from the camera below the nose of the plane, with the
  sensor symbols on it. The ground station can point the camera.

Term: SITL
  Software in the loop. ArduPilot runs on your computer, not on a flight controller. The sim
  gives it the physics and the sensors of the plane.

Term: Tactical map
  The map of the valley in the ground station, on the screen with the title TACTICAL
  SITUATION. It shows the plane, home, the plan and the mission on the vehicle.

Term: Waypoint
  A point that the plane flies to, at a set height. Most items in a mission are waypoints.

Term: T1-S Ranger
  The plain plane of the sim (`--aircraft t1s-ranger`): two motors, a V-tail, and only a
  receiver and two ESCs, so it has no flight controller and one flight mode, MANUAL. You
  throw it by hand and fly it from the keyboard or a radio.

Term: T1-S VTOL
  The other plane of the sim, and its default (`--aircraft t1s-vtol`): the T1-S airframe with
  two tilt-rotors and a rear rotor, and a flight controller. ArduPilot flies it, and you fly
  it from the ground control station.

Term: VTOL
  An aircraft that takes off and lands vertically (Vertical Take-Off and Landing), like a
  copter, and flies forward on its wing, like a plane. The T1-S VTOL is one.

Term: tilt-rotor
  A motor that can turn between pointing up and pointing forward. In a hover it lifts the
  plane like a copter rotor. In forward flight it pushes the plane like the motor of a plane.

Term: transition
  The change between a hover and forward flight. The plane does it while it flies: the wing
  motors tilt, and the wing takes the weight from the rotors, or gives it back.

Term: Computer pilot
  An opponent in the games that the sim flies. Each computer pilot flies a T1-S on the same
  physics as your plane, and has its own skill. Kestrel is an ace, Merlin flies at a club
  level and Swift is a rookie.

Term: Pylon race
  A race round a triangle of three [[pylons]], as in the pylon racing leagues. G starts one.
  Five laps make a race. The first pilot across the start line after the last lap wins.

Term: Pylon
  A tall marker on the field. In a [[pylon race]], the three pylons mark the turns of the
  course. Fly round the outside of each one.

Term: Cut
  A turn inside a pylon in a [[pylon race]]. One cut adds 10 % to your time. Two cuts
  disqualify you.

Term: Tag
  A game where one plane is [[it]] and chases the others. When it touches another plane, that
  plane is it. In the simulator, T starts one. The [[runner]] plays one with ArduPilot planes.

Term: It
  The plane that chases the others in a game of [[tag]]. The pilot who is it for the least
  time wins.

Term: Arena
  The part of the sky for a game of [[tag]]: a circle of 90 m round the centre of the field (the [[arena centre]], for the runner),
  below 60 m. Small cones mark its edge. If you fly out of it, you are [[it]].

Term: Flight computer
  The device between the TX15 and the autopilot. The TX15 sends it the stick and switch channels on a serial line. It passes them on to [[ArduPilot]] as the radio channels, and it sends the plane's state back to the TX15. In the sim, a simulated flight computer does this.

Term: CRSF
  The serial protocol of ExpressLRS radio links. The TX15 and the [[flight computer]] use it on their serial line: channels one way, telemetry and MAVLink the other.

Term: Agent plan
  What the [[ground control agent]] tells a plane to do for the next few seconds. It has a
  [[primitive]], a target or a point, a plan id and a time to live. The ground sends it as a
  MAVLink `COMMAND_INT` with the command 31010. Only a plane in [[AGENT mode]] takes it.

Term: AGENT mode
  A flight mode of our ArduPlane fork, custom mode 50. In AGENT mode, the [[executor]] flies
  the [[agent plan]] from the ground. The [[safety envelope]] keeps the plane safe. A change of
  the mode switch on the radio takes the plane out of AGENT mode at once.

Term: Executor
  The code in the plane that flies an [[agent plan]]: `cognition_executor`, a Rust library. It
  turns the plan into a bank, a pitch and a throttle, 50 times a second. The same code runs in
  ArduPilot, in SITL and in the tests.

Term: Safety envelope
  The part of the [[executor]] that checks each demand before the plane flies it. It keeps the
  plane inside the [[fence]], between the [[floor]] and the [[ceiling]], clear of other aircraft
  and above the [[stall margin]]. A plan cannot switch it off.

Term: Fence
  The circle that the [[safety envelope]] keeps the plane inside: a geofence. `AGENT_FENCE_N`
  and `AGENT_FENCE_E` set its centre in metres from home, and `AGENT_FENCE_R` sets its radius.
  If `AGENT_FENCE_R` is 0, the circle geofence of ArduPilot is used.

Term: Floor
  The lowest height above the ground that the [[safety envelope]] allows, set with
  `AGENT_FLOOR`. Below it, the plane climbs before it flies the plan again. On a flat field,
  the height above the ground and the height above home are the same.

Term: Ceiling
  The highest height above home that the [[safety envelope]] allows, set with `AGENT_CEIL`.

Term: Separation
  The smallest distance to another aircraft that the [[safety envelope]] allows, set with
  `AGENT_SEP`. The envelope keeps a buffer round it, of at least 3 m. Across or above and
  below, the larger distance counts.

Term: Stall margin
  The airspeed that the plane must keep over `AIRSPEED_MIN`, set with `AGENT_ASPD_MRG`. Below
  `AIRSPEED_MIN` plus this margin, the [[safety envelope]] gets the airspeed back first.

Term: Track
  Another aircraft as the plane knows it: its id, its position, its velocity and the time
  since the plane heard it. The ground sends tracks as `ADSB_VEHICLE` messages. The plane
  ignores a track that it has not heard for 3 s.

Term: Ground control agent
  The program on the ground that decides an [[agent plan]] for each plane, about once a
  second. Its brain can be a script, a trained network or a person. The scripted brain plays
  [[tag]] as the [[computer pilot]]s do.

Term: Primitive
  The kind of [[agent plan]]: [[pursue]], [[evade]], [[go to]], [[orbit]], [[band]] or
  [[loiter]]. AG_PRIM gives it as a number from 1 to 6, and 0 for no plan.

Term: Pursue
  A [[primitive]], number 1: chase the [[track]] that the plan names, and stay on its tail,
  just outside the [[separation]].

Term: Evade
  A [[primitive]], number 2: break away from the [[track]] that the plan names. The plane flies
  fast, away from the threat and 20 m above or below it.

Term: Go to
  A [[primitive]], number 3: fly to the point of the plan, then circle it.

Term: Orbit
  A [[primitive]], number 4: circle the point of the plan, at its radius and its altitude.

Term: Band
  A [[primitive]], number 5: stay between two heights and circle over the arena. The altitude
  of the plan is the bottom of the band. The radius of the plan is its thickness.

Term: Fallback
  What the plane does when it cannot fly its [[agent plan]]. AG_FALL tells you why: 1 when the
  plan ran out or its target went silent, 2 for bad input, 3 for a [[recovery]].

Term: Recovery
  A [[fallback]], number 3: the plane is below the [[floor]] or the [[stall margin]]. It gets
  its height or its airspeed back first. Then it flies the plan again.

Term: Intervention
  A step where the [[safety envelope]] changes what the plan asks for. AG_ENV counts the
  interventions since the plane entered [[AGENT mode]].

Term: Time to live
  How long an [[agent plan]] is good for, in seconds, from when the plane gets it. If no new
  plan comes in this time, the plane circles where it is.

Term: Agent status
  What a plane in [[AGENT mode]] tells the ground once a second, as four `NAMED_VALUE_INT`
  messages. AG_PLAN is the id of its plan, AG_PRIM the [[primitive]] of that plan, AG_ENV the
  count of [[intervention]]s and AG_FALL the [[fallback]]. The console of the [[runner]] shows
  them as `plan`, `prim`, `env` and `fall`.

Term: Runner
  `cognition_runner`, a program that flies three ArduPlane [[SITL]] planes with no window,
  faster than real time. With `--agent`, the [[ground control agent]] flies them in a game of
  [[tag]]. With `--console` as well, you send [[agent plan]]s to the first plane after the
  game. Type `plan`, `again`, `mode`, `wait`, `watch`, `status`, `reset` or `quit`.

Term: Arena centre
  The centre of the arena of a game of [[tag]] in the [[runner]], and of the [[fence]] of
  [[AGENT mode]]: 70 m north of home. The arena is 90 m round it. The fence is 85 m round it,
  so the plane turns back before it leaves the arena.

Term: Game command
  A MAVLink `COMMAND_INT` with the command 31011 that tells a plane the state of the game of
  [[tag]]: its role, the time left and the plane that it cannot tag back. A plane takes it in any
  mode.

Term: COMMAND_ACK
  The MAVLink reply to a command. `result` is 0 when the plane accepts it, and 2 when the plane
  refuses it. For an [[agent plan]], `result_param2` gives the reason of a refusal.

Term: EKF
  The extended Kalman filter of ArduPilot. It estimates the position, the velocity and the
  attitude of the plane from its sensors.

Term: League
  The program that makes the [[executor]] better by itself: `cognition_league`. It keeps the
  [[champion]], runs [[generation]]s of [[challenger]]s, and writes what it did in the folder
  `league`.

Term: Champion
  The best executor that the [[league]] found so far: a [[tuning]], tactics and code.

Term: Challenger
  A new executor that a [[strategy]] gives to the [[league]], to play against the
  [[champion]].

Term: Generation
  One turn of the [[league]]: the strategies give challengers, the [[fast arena]] plays them,
  and the league promotes the best one that passes, or none.

Term: Strategy
  A way to make [[challenger]]s: the evolution strategy, the Claude tuner or the Claude
  engineer.

Term: Tuning
  The numbers that set how the [[executor]] flies its primitives: the gains of its guidance,
  its height and speed control, and how it pursues and evades. Each number has limits. The
  [[safety envelope]] is not in the tuning.

Term: Fast arena
  A game of [[tag]] with no window and no [[SITL]], many times faster than real time: the
  executor and three [[computer pilot]]s on the flight model of the sim. `cognition_arena`.

Term: Base
  In [[Towers]], the place where the drones of a team start and come back to. It is at one end
  of the field. A team sees 150 m round its base.

Term: Tower
  In [[Towers]], a radio tower on the field. A team that holds a tower gets one point each
  second from it, and it sees 200 m round it. A team takes a tower when its drones fly in the
  [[circle]] of the tower.

Term: Circle
  In [[Towers]], the area round a [[tower]] where a drone counts for it: within 60 m of the
  tower, and not more than 120 m high. Only a drone that flies counts.

Term: Control
  In [[Towers]], how far a [[tower]] is towards each team, from −100 % (Red) to +100 % (Blue).
  A team holds the tower when the control gets to its end. The tower becomes neutral when the
  control gets back to 0.

Term: Fog
  In [[Towers]], the parts of the field that a team does not see. An enemy drone in the fog is
  not on the map of the team, or it is where the team last saw it.

Term: Seat
  In [[Towers]], the place of a commander of a team on the game server. A computer commander
  or a person at the commander app sits in it. A seat gets only the map of its team.

Term: Captain
  The computer in the seat of a team in [[Towers]]: `recreation_towers::Commander`. It reads
  the map of its team and gives each drone an order. The [[league]] makes better captains.

Term: PreArm
  A check that ArduPilot does before it arms. If a check fails, ArduPilot does not arm, and it
  sends a message that starts with "PreArm:" and says why.

Term: ExpressLRS
  An open radio link between a transmitter, such as the [[TX15]], and the receiver in a plane.
  It uses the CRSF protocol, and it carries the telemetry back to the transmitter.

Term: Uplink quality
  The share of the frames from the [[TX15]] that get to the plane, from 0 % to 100 %. The
  screen of the TX15 shows it.

Term: Contested
  In [[Towers]], a [[circle]] with drones of the two teams in it. Its control moves only for
  the team with more drones. The map makes a contested circle flash.

Term: Scout
  In [[Towers]], a drone that flies to see the field, not to take a tower. In the rules, the
  Red scout is the first Red drone.

Term: Seed
  The number that starts the random parts of a game in the [[fast arena]]. The same seed plays
  the same game, so the [[league]] plays a champion and a challenger on the same seeds.

Term: Home tower
  In [[Towers]], the [[tower]] next to the [[base]] of a team. Each team holds its home tower
  at the start. The centre tower and the two flank towers, west and east, start neutral.

Term: Lane
  In [[Towers]], a path 60 m wide that is cut through the forest. Three lanes, west, mid and
  east, go from the [[home tower]] of each team to the home tower of the other team, through a
  tower on the way. A drone can fly [[low]] along a lane, below the treetops.

Term: Low
  In [[Towers]], a drone that flies below the treetops, at 16 m to 20 m. A drone that flies low
  goes along the [[lane]]s, and the trees hide it. A drone that flies high goes straight over
  the forest, at 55 m to 75 m, and it is easy to see.

Term: Clearing
  In [[Towers]], an open area in the forest where a drone can circle [[low]]: round each
  [[tower]], round each [[base]], and over the lake.
